{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789232962,"nonce":"sgUa2wxEsdmnQ9xRWj7LEjF6VEk=","title":"Integer arithmetic declared wider than its operands is computed at the operand width","message":"mut i = 1              // i32, the literal default\n    xs.push(i * i)         // into a List\u003ci64\u003e\n\n`i * i` is recorded as an i64 result while both operands are i32, so\nthe multiply is emitted at 32 bits and the 8-byte store that follows\nwrites four bytes of product and four of whatever the register held:\n`build(100)[99]` read back -4294957495, whose low word is 9801.\n\n`normalize_int_binary_operands` only reconciles the two operands with\neach other. The result's declared width was never consulted.\n\nFix: after operand normalisation, an integer operation whose declared\nresult is wider than its operands widens both to the result type\nfirst; one whose declared result is narrower takes the operands'\ntype. Comparisons are left alone, their result is a bool.","files":null}]}